cks and he will  use disruptive  uh cyber uh messaging  but also  shut down and and he's used malware as  well as ransomware  um he's been connected to those kinds of  attacks in our country as well as others  so i think on the cyber front yes that  there could be uh attacks back we have  the capability to attack back and i  think that is one of the tools in the  toolbox so you think a military conflict  uh on the ground is unlikely that's my  view but i cannot say that i would bet  the ranch on it as we would say in texas  um i don't know if he would really um  be able to convince his people  that  it would be worth  killing russian soldiers the ukraines  would lose but also russia would as well  and i don't think he would want that so  i would i would err on the side that he  wouldn't do that but i do think he will  do a lot of damage in other ways  if there is going to be a way out of  this uh  in the diplomatic  sphere  would one way of starting this be simply  for nato to rule out the possibility of  ukraine joining nato  i do not think that is an option  because  everyone  is is i think unified in saying  that  it would be such a sign of weakness  for nato to pledge to russia  that we wouldn't take in any other  country that seeks  membership in our nato alliance i don't  think we would ever do it and  i think it would be the wrong signal  what we need to do is show strength  i think that's what putin understands is  strength and unity and uh  there's no way that we would sit back  and let putin then have a veto over a  country that qualifies the last point um  do you think the united states could  help  to as it were stiffen europe's spine by  saying for example it will guarantee gas  supplies to europe  in the event that the russians decided  to put the squeeze on energy supplies  from siberia to the western europe we  definitely need to do that that is  something that has been  talked about but i think now is the time  for action uh we have  fun abundance  energy resources in america and natural  gas that we could export but we have to  be able to get the receiving facilities  in our european countries and there have  been  many obstacles to those receiving  facilities being built but we can  provide affordable natural gas which is  clean energy  to our european allies and that's what  we ought to be promoting much more now  uh that we have seen that russia really  will use gas as a leverage and that is  the next step that i think uh will come  up in congress and i hope that the  administration will work with congress  to  uh start promoting natural gas exports  ambassador thank you so much for your  time trevor thank you it's been great to  visit with you  speaking at the security conference in  munich yesterday boris johnson said an  invasion of ukraine would send a shock  around the world  he said that severe economic sanctions  will be imposed if one does take place  but is that going to be enough to deter  vladimir putin joining me now is simon  fraser who's a former head of the  foreign office  good morning simon um good morning  you're a seasoned diplomat you've seen a  lot of this um are we really facing the  biggest land war in europe as described  by the prime minister  well of course it's always difficult to  disentangle the disinformation from the  from the facts but it does seem that  actually contrary to what a lot of the  experts have been predicting over recent  weeks  we may well be moving in that direction  and we do have to take very seriously  the warnings that are being issued by  president biden and the prime minister  and others now i think so the situation  does look pretty uh alarming at the  moment  the um the conference is still going on  but the russians aren't there um  can there be a diplomatic solution if  they just aren't even turning up  well it's true that they're not there  and i think that what that tells us is  that  president putin is not at the moment  really interested in negotiation and a  negotiated solution uh he's made demands  which he knew nato and the americans  could not accept he's kept the door open  a bit through uh the discussions of the  activity of his foreign minister lavrov  but it doesn't seem to me at the moment  that there is a very active disposition  to negotiate on the russian side and i  mean there are ways in which  negotiations could be taken forward  if both sides wanted to do it but one  thing's really important to say we can't  negotiate with russia if russia has  broken international law has infringed  the sovereignty of ukraine  and has invaded so the window for  diplomacy at the moment the current  window for diplomacy does look as if it  may be closing  taking all of that together do you agree  with the europe minister james cleverley  when he says as he did earlier in this  program  that uh an invasion is more likely today  than not likely  i just don't know the answer to that i  mean if you  you know if you look at this rationally  from our perspective you would say that  the risks for putin of doing that are  immense  and he's going to incur huge economic  political military and human cost  if he does that  but what we have to be always aware of  is that other people don't always see  the world through the same perspective  as us and we have to understand the  different motivators that are driving uh  president putin to this action uh even  if we don't agree with them we have to  try and understand them uh i can't tell  you exactly what's going on in his head  and what he's gonna do i think we do  have to take the risk seriously  well what's going on his head as you say  uh is a bit of a a mystery but um the  foreign secretary this morning appears  to  have  better intelligence she says that putin  won't stop at ukraine and that he has  designed on the baltics and elsewhere  um  if she's right that means that this  crisis  has to escalate and at some point there  is going to be actual conflict  yes i think what he's trying to do is a  number of things he's trying to do he's  basically trying to rewrite  the sort of european security structures  that we've had since the cold war which  he believes have been shameful for  russia a reverse for russia he's trying  to reintegrate ukraine into russia  because that he believes is something he  wrote a long essay on that recently and  it's something he believes in  he's trying to weaken america and divide  the west and he's trying of course to  bolster uh russian uh control through  force in the near abroad all those are  recognized objectives of russian policy  and what he's trying to do actually is  exploit a moment of what he sees to be  weakness in the west and distraction in  the west in america and in europe to  achieve those goals so there is a risk  that he will act in ukraine and of  course we must be aware that he may have  broader uh objectives that's why  deterring him from this action would  have been uh ideal and if we can't deter  him we are going to need a very strong  united powerful and effective response  to try and limit any further action  if you were in the foreign office now  how important would you regard what k  bailey hutchinson has just  been saying about the need to reduce  european dependence on russian energy  supplies uh what she seemed to be saying  is that the americans ought to be  ramping up their provision of energy to  europe and building facilities so that  you europe can be if you like um  stronger  in facing down putin  well i mean there's a whole series of  lessons that we need to draw uh one is  of course about europe's dependence for  its security on the united states that's  a long running discussion another is  this very important issue about energy  dependency and of course we should be  seeking to address that but let's not  fool ourselves it's not something that  could be solved overnight there is a  whole sort of infrastructure of energy  supply to europe from uh russia  and from the east which you can't just  switch off  easily because you can't necessarily  replace those resources easily there is  of course a lot going on now at looking  at alternative supplies of liquid gas  for example but you have to have the  infrastructure to be able to do that so  yes definitely we should look at it but  it's a serious challenge  simon fraser thank you so much for your  time this morning  thank you  understandably we focused on events to  the east this morning but that doesn't  mean that it's all quiet on the home  front  with may's northern ireland assembly  elections looming current polling  suggests that shin fein could become the  largest party  that would mean that its vice president  michelle o'neill could become first  minister  sky's david blevins has been talking to  her about her vision for northern  ireland  michelle o'neill  you would never accept a trade border  between northern ireland and the  republic so why should unionists accept  one in the irish sea between northern  ireland and the rest of the uk  well dude i don't want to see any  borders we've said from day one that  brexit wasn't compatible with our peace  agreement with our good friday agreement  i've never wanted to see the  interruptions that we're now  experiencing today i think many of us  here warned against the dangers of  brexit and what it actually would mean  for us in reality and we're facing those  challenges today but we sought along  with the majority of other political  parties sought to find mitigation  against the worst impact of brexit that  comes in the form of the protocol so it  protects our peace agreement it protects  the good for the agreement it prevents a  hard border on the island of ireland and  it ensures the continuity of trade  across this island which is obviously  flourishing as a direct result of the  protocol even if we accept that it's  there as a result of brexit you say it  protects the peace process but it has  recently led to the resignation of the  first minister so if it is destabilizing  politics here  isn't a time for you to urge the eu to  find something that wins cross-community  support  well it was never across community  support for brexit the majority of  people here the majority of political  parties on across community bases voted  to reject brexit yet in all it's forced  it upon us this tory government boris  johnson has shown at every turn that  they do not care less for the people who  live here it is in fact the british  government that negotiated the protocol  it is in fact then the signed up to an  international treaty which they have  been putting in jeopardy ever since the  the ink was barely dry on the agreement  itself so we are not in an ideal  situation however the protocol does give  us the best protection and does afford  us a lot of opportunity what can you say  to reassure unionists that the protocol  is the best solution  can you reassure them that it is in your  view just a trade border and that you  respect the position of northern ireland  constitutionally as part of the united  kingdom i signed up to the good friday  agreement so it'll be the people here  alone that will decide if there's  constitutional change there'll never be  anything forested upon people i think  unfortunately for the unionist people  for that wider civic society the  unionist leaders have actually completed  the issue of the protocol and brexit a  mess which they single-handedly  delivered alongside their friends in the  tories and but they have completed that  issue of identity with the issue of  trade which comes about as direct result  of brexit so there are two distinctly  different things there's going to be an  election the polls suggest michelle  o'neill  may be northern ireland's next first  minister  given that the ira campaign  is still in people's memories here it's  not that long ago is northern ireland  ready for a sinn fein first minister  i think it's even crazy that you're even  asking me that question because i think  it'll be the people that will decide who  will there who they will elect who they  will they will decide who will be the  largest party like i come from a  background or a republican you know i'm  a proud republican i'm somebody who i  believe can govern for all people not  just those republican tradition but  those from other traditions so to sit  here in 2022 for anybody to question a  nationalist ability to be first minister  to hold a position in the legal  profession you know to hold the position  of justice ministry is just absolutely  madness those days are gone but but it's  not just any nationalist politician it  is for many people a party that is  still considered to be the political  wing of the ira no matter how long we  are past ceasefires and good friday  agreement so what do you say to victims  of ira violence for example who would  still have a difficulty with the shin  fian first minister  i think that we're a society that comes  from conflict we've had a very difficult  past we have to recognize that we've had  a very difficult past we also have to  recognize that we've all had a very  different experience of the past my  lived experience would be very different  perhaps to someone else of my uh my  generation some of my peers but we have  to recognize that we also have to deal  with the past so this british government  that are intent on trying to cover over  what they did here isn't a good  situation because how do we build for a  better future if we don't properly deal  with the past you talk about the british  trying to cover up the past you're  obviously talking about the proposed  amnesty and historical prosecution of  british army veterans  what's the difference though between  those veterans and and former  paramilitaries be they republican or  loyalist who walked free after the good  friday agreement or who were granted in  the case of many in the ira pardons  well firstly again i mean i do not  support in any shape or form an honesty  of any form for anybody so let me be  very clear about that i am 45 years of  age i'm somebody who has been about the  peace process from this inception i'm  someone who believes in the good freddie  agreement i'm somebody who believes in  dealing with the past so that we all can  move forward together the british  government have tried to block every  step forward in dealing with the past  because for those to say draw a line  under it forget that it happened that  doesn't work the impact of conflict is  intergenerational so today's generation  are still fighting the battles of the  past i don't want myself that for my  children i don't want it for my  grandchildren i don't want it for  anybody else's children if unionists  attempt to block uh shin fan first  minister by refusing to nominate a  deputy is it time to consider voluntary  coalition so that one of the smaller  parties could nominate a deputy first  minister  well firstly let me say this the days of  democracy and the terms of unionism  alone are gone and they're never going  to return so it'll be the people alone  in this election that will decide who  they wish to govern for them and when it  comes to whether or not there should be  a different form of government you have  to remember that we are society coming  out of conflict you have to remember  that we have special and unique  circumstances here so we can't be  looking towards different systems right  now the good for the agreement was an  agreement that a lot of people put a lot  of hard work into nationalism unionist  republican and loyalist a lot of sweat  went in to bring about a peace agreement  that's something that i hold very dearly  and something that i will protect at all  turns so i believe in the system of  governance as a necessary piece of  governance because of where we've come  from  there's a lot of speculation that you  will be first minister of northern  ireland and that your party leader mary  lou mcdonald will be the next taoiseach  the next irish prime minister  if that scenario arises  do you think that immediately opens the  door to your referendum on irish unity  well i certainly think that we're closer  than we ever have been previously and i  think now's the time to plan and that's  why we're saying to the irish government  now is the time for them to make the  preparation let's not fall into the  traps that have been fallen into in  terms of brexit make the preparation  let's start to have a healthy  conversation about the things that  matter every day to people what does the  health service look like in a united  ireland what does education look like in  united ireland so i think that this is a  hopeful conversation because our society  here has lived apart for far too long  because if the last couple of years of  brexit has told us anything is that the  tories will never prioritize the  interests of people here whether you be  unionist nationalist or other earlier  this week people were surprised to hear  your party leader send platinum jubilee  greetings to the queen we've come a long  way from the days when jerry adams was  describing her as mrs windsor  was that just electioneering a reaching  out to the unionist community or the  moderate middle ground community or do  you genuinely respect queen elizabeth of  course i mean i think we have to i mean  we live in a society that's been so  divided and i respect the views of those  of unions that entity those with the  british identity and those people that  look towards the monarchy it's not my  thing but that's okay i can be  respectful enough to understand that for  those for other people in my society who  my community that is important to them  so that's why we sent our best wishes  and you know i do so again today on your  program  michelle o'neill thanks very much thank  you  that's it from us for this week for the  main program  brexit northern ireland covid  warren europe who is it that said that  there's no drama and tension in modern  politics well to be honest i'll be glad  if i can be back here next week  reporting to you that in ukraine there's  nothing much to see  after the break i'll be talking to sam  coates about our big interviews see you  again next week  [Music]  you
 

